Subject: [PATCH 8/9] add_fault's docstring: the slip at a sharp turn's apex
 can be spurious

The fault's constraint holds the two sides together along one normal at
each vertex. At a sharp turn that normal cannot match both arms, and the
apex can open against them: at a 150-degree hairpin (regular 1/12 box,
sampled 0.3 cells apart) the jump at the apex is 0.131 and 0.098 along the
two arms' normals, and the slip reported there is 0.134 against 0.086 for
the same fault on a mesh four times finer, where the apex hardly moves;
a cell away the two agree. The docstring says to read slip more than
about a cell from a sharp turn. Text only.

Underworld development team with AI support from Claude Code
---
 src/underworld3/utilities/fault_split.py | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/underworld3/utilities/fault_split.py b/src/underworld3/utilities/fault_split.py
index 6239f2cc..d782203d 100644
--- a/src/underworld3/utilities/fault_split.py
+++ b/src/underworld3/utilities/fault_split.py
@@ -1917,6 +1917,15 @@ def add_fault(mesh, faults, verbose=False, cut=True, exclude=None,
         farther from its chain than half the local cell side (the base mesh's,
         before the cut). A chain that cuts across a corner by less than half a
         cell is added.
+
+    Notes
+    -----
+    A turn of the trace is a vertex of the fault, and the fault's constraint
+    holds the two sides together along ONE normal there. At a sharp turn that
+    normal cannot match both arms, and the apex can open against them: at a
+    150-degree hairpin the slip reported at the apex vertex was 1.6 times the
+    refined fault's largest slip, while a cell away the two agreed. Read a
+    fault's slip more than about a cell from its sharp turns.
     """
     from enum import Enum
 
